---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<DocsLayout title="Workflows" description="Configure, plan, run, resume, and score scmle projects." current="workflows">
  <p class="eyebrow"><span></span> The working loop</p>
  <h1>Make the workflow<br /><em>deliberate.</em></h1>
  <p class="lead">A project declaration is a decision record. It says what the target means, when a feature is available, how rows may be split, how much compute is allowed, and what evidence must exist before a result is used.</p>

  <h2>Declare the problem</h2>
  <p>The examples on this page run as written in an empty folder. To follow along, write a small table of orders, with a column (<code>renewal_reason</code>) that is only known after the outcome:</p>
  <pre><code class="language-python">{`from pathlib import Path

import numpy
import pandas

rng = numpy.random.default_rng(0)
rows = 1200
orders = pandas.DataFrame(
    {
        "account_id": rng.integers(0, 150, rows),
        "observed_at": pandas.date_range("2025-01-01", periods=rows, freq="6h"),
        "seats": rng.integers(1, 50, rows),
        "usage": rng.gamma(2.0, 10.0, rows).round(1),
        "tickets": rng.poisson(2, rows),
    }
)
score = 0.04 * orders.usage - 0.3 * orders.tickets + rng.normal(size=rows)
orders["renewed"] = (score > 0).astype(int)
orders["renewal_reason"] = numpy.where(orders.renewed == 1, "renewed", "lapsed")
Path("data").mkdir(exist_ok=True)
orders.iloc[:1000].to_parquet("data/orders.parquet")
new = orders.iloc[1000:].drop(columns=["renewed", "renewal_reason"])
new.to_parquet("data/new_orders.parquet")`}</code></pre>
  <pre><code class="language-python">from scmle.api.project import Project

project = Project(
    data="data/orders.parquet",
    target="renewed",
    task="binary_classification",
    metric="roc_auc",
    group_column="account_id",
    time_column="observed_at",
    unavailable_at_prediction=["renewal_reason"],
    validation="group",
    time_budget=900,
    output_dir="runs/orders",
)</code></pre>
  <p>The important fields are the constraints around the model. <code>group_column</code> prevents one account leaking across splits. <code>time_column</code> preserves ordering. <code>unavailable_at_prediction</code> makes post-outcome columns explicit instead of relying on a reviewer to notice them later.</p>

  <h2>Plan before you spend compute</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">scmle plan data/orders.parquet \
  --target renewed \
  --task binary_classification \
  --validation group \
  --group account_id \
  --unavailable renewal_reason</code></pre>
  <p>A plan is the cheapest place to catch an unclear target, an incompatible split, missing labels, or a dataset too large for the selected training mode. Treat it as a reviewable artifact in a pull request.</p>

  <h2>Run, resume, predict</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">{`scmle run data/orders.parquet --target renewed --validation group --group account_id \\
  --unavailable renewal_reason --budget 120 --output-dir runs/orders
run=$(ls -d runs/orders/run_* | tail -n 1)    # for example runs/orders/run_20261015_001
scmle resume "$run"            # refused here: this run finished
echo "exit code: $?"
scmle predict "$run/model" data/new_orders.parquet --trusted --output predictions.csv`}</code></pre>
  <p>Runs are bounded and restartable. If a worker or host disappears, resume from the run directory (a finished run, like the one above, is refused with exit code 1) rather than starting a second experiment with a different implicit state. Predictions should be made from the packaged bundle, not from an estimator reconstructed by hand. <code>--trusted</code> loads this bundle because it is unsigned and your own; set <code>SCMLE_SIGNING_KEY</code> to sign and verify bundles instead (see the <a href={`${base}/docs/cli/#bundles`}>command line</a>).</p>
  <p>The full command set is <code>plan</code>, <code>run</code>, <code>resume</code>, <code>catalog</code>, <code>predict</code>, <code>serve</code>, <code>export-onnx</code>, and <code>package</code>; see <code>scmle --help</code>.</p>

  <h2>Choose the task explicitly when it matters</h2>
  <p>Supported task requests include binary and multiclass classification, regression, survival, ordinal, ranking, multilabel, multioutput, forecast, and uplift. <code>auto</code> is useful for exploration; a named task is better for a release because it documents the intended semantics.</p>
  <div class="callout"><p><strong>Review rule:</strong> a higher validation score does not override a bad split, an unavailable feature, a weak label definition, or an unreviewed curation finding.</p></div>
</DocsLayout>
